<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pannai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pannai was a port polity on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>. It is mentioned among the places attacked during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> naval raids of the eleventh century. Its subordination to Chola power and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a> pressure weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a>'s centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pannai.jpg" alt="Pannai" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolaes Visscher II · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolaes_Visscher_Indiae_Orientalis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Pannai, also written Panai or Pane, was a Buddhist kingdom that existed between the 11th and 14th centuries in the valleys of the Barumun and Panai rivers, in today's Labuhan Batu and South Tapanuli regencies. Historians suggest it was a principality or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> within the Srivijayan mandala and later within that of the Dharmasraya kingdom. It is first named in the Tamil Thanjavur inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola I</a>, dated 1025 and 1030, as one of the cities taken in his campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, together with Malaiyur, Ilamuri-Desam and Kadaram; according to the inscription it fell to a surprise attack from the rear, launched from Srivijaya, the mandala's capital, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> already held.</p><p>Three centuries later Pane appears in canto 13 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarakretagama">Nagarakretagama</a>, written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-prapanca">Mpu Prapanca</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> in 1365, among the Sumatran kingdoms under Majapahit influence. Javanese overlordship there probably began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a>'s Pamalayu expedition, which drew Malayu Dharmasraya and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> into the Singhasari mandala.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The only local source is the Panai inscription, found near the Bahal temple complex in Padang Lawas, much of it unreadable. Its mention of a haji and a kuti suggests a small kingdom ruled by a Buddhist lesser king, the haji being a regional or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> ruler beneath a paramount maharaja. The land was probably rich in forest products, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a>, and in livestock, and may have yielded gold; Armenian travel records name Pane as a port for high-quality camphor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1846 the geologist Franz Junghuhn reported the temple compounds of Padanglawas, upstream on the Barumun River: 16 Vajrayana Buddhist temples of red brick, most now in ruins, of which the Bahal temple is the best preserved. Because the dry plain could not support farming villages, the kingdom's people probably lived nearer the river mouths. A bronze Buddha Amitabha in Sri Lankan style from the temple of Pamutung is now in the Tropenmuseum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pannai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
